      "clause": "True minimum versus supplied rounded-input proxy",
      "status": "approved_narrow_correction",
      "lean_declarations": [
        "SafeLearning.CompleteAppliedGaussianChanceConstraint.actual_gaussian_point_ninety_five_constraint_has_the_true_smallest_threshold",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteAppliedGaussianChanceConstraint.actual_source_threshold_true_rounding_and_rounded_input_proxy_distinction",
        "SafeLearning.CompleteAppliedGaussianQuantile.actual_supplied_five_decimal_quantile_is_a_rounding_and_not_the_exact_root"
      ],
      "reason": "For the actual Gaussian(3,6) law, the probability requirement holds iff the threshold is at least 3+sqrt(6) times the true .95 CDF root. The source-matched IsLeast theorem establishes this minimum; the direct rounding theorem establishes nearest-four 7.0291 for that value and 7.0290 for the proxy formed from 1.64485. The proxy is strictly smaller, hence fails the exact .95 requirement by the same iff. The replacement exposes these exact distinctions without changing the given Gaussian model or probability level."
    },
    {
      "clause": "Two-sided radius 1.96 is rounded",
      "status": "approved_narrow_correction",
      "lean_declarations": [
        "SafeLearning.CompleteAppliedGaussianCentralConfidence.actual_two_sided_ninety_five_probability_and_one_point_nine_six_rounding"
      ],
      "reason": "The actual central95% radius is strictly below 1.96 and rounds to it at two decimals; its true central event has probability .95 while radius1.96 has strictly larger probability. Calling 1.96 a rounded radius is therefore precise."
    }
  ],
